195	
196	Node variables represent outputs produced by nodes during execution. They are read-only and referenced via `$vars.{nodeId}.{outputId}`.
197	
198	When you add a node via `uip maestro flow node add`, node variables are created automatically. When using `Edit` against the `.flow` file, add them manually.
199	
200	### Schema
201	
202	```typescript
203	{
204	  id: string              // Format: "{nodeId}.{outputId}"
205	  type?: string           // Output type
206	  subType?: string        // For complex types
207	  schema?: object         // JSON Schema for structured outputs
208	  description?: string    // What this output contains
209	  binding: {
210	    nodeId: string        // Source node ID
211	    outputId: string      // Output port ID (e.g., "output", "error")
212	  }
213	}
214	```
215	
216	### Example
217	
218	```json
219	{
220	  "variables": {
221	    "nodes": [
222	      {
223	        "id": "fetchData.output",
224	        "type": "object",
225	        "description": "HTTP response body",
226	        "binding": {
227	          "nodeId": "fetchData",
228	          "outputId": "output"
229	        }
230	      },
231	      {
232	        "id": "fetchData.error",
233	        "type": "object",
234	        "description": "Error details if the request fails",
235	        "schema": {
236	          "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
237	          "type": "object",
238	          "required": ["code", "message"],
239	          "properties": {
240	            "code": { "type": "string" },
241	            "message": { "type": "string" },
242	            "detail": { "type": "string" },
243	            "category": { "type": "string" },
244	            "status": { "type": "integer" }
245	          }
246	        },
247	        "binding": {
248	          "nodeId": "fetchData",
249	          "outputId": "error"
250	        }
251	      }
252	    ]
253	  }
254	}
255	```
256	
257	---
258	
259	## Variable Updates (`variableUpdates`)
260	
261	Variable updates assign new values to `inout` (state) variables at specific nodes. They execute when the node completes.
262	
263	### Schema
264	
265	```typescript
266	{
267	  "variableUpdates": {
268	    "{nodeId}": [
269	      {
270	        "variableId": string,    // ID of the inout variable to update
271	        "expression": string     // =js: expression to evaluate and assign
272	      }
273	    ]
274	  }
275	}
276	```
277	
278	### Example
279	
280	```json
281	{
282	  "variables": {
283	    "globals": [
284	      {
285	        "id": "counter",
286	        "direction": "inout",
287	        "type": "number",
288	        "defaultValue": 0
289	      },
290	      {
291	        "id": "lastStatus",
292	        "direction": "inout",
293	        "type": "string",
294	        "defaultValue": "pending"
295	      }
296	    ],
297	    "variableUpdates": {
298	      "processItem": [
299	        {
300	          "variableId": "counter",
301	          "expression": "=js:$vars.counter + 1"
302	        },
303	        {
304	          "variableId": "lastStatus",
305	          "expression": "=js:$vars.processItem.output.status"
306	        }
307	      ]
308	    }
309	  }
310	}
311	```
312	
313	> **Only `inout` variables can be updated.** Updating an `in` or `out` variable is invalid.
